                "content": "**1.  NMD\u2011substrate prediction**\n\n| Construct | Position of the premature stop codon (PTC) | Down\u2011stream exon\u2013exon junction(s) that remain in the mature RNA | Distance PTC \u2192 nearest downstream junction |\u202f>\u202f50\u202fnt ? | NMD substrate? |\n|-----------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|----------|----------------|\n| **A**     | Exon\u202f4, 90\u202fnt upstream of the exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f5 junction | exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f5 (and later exon\u202f5\u2011exon\u202f6) | 90\u202fnt | **Yes** | **Yes** |\n| **B**     | Same PTC as in A (still 90\u202fnt upstream of the original exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f5 site) | Because exon\u202f5 is skipped, the only downstream junction is exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f6 | 35\u202fnt | No | **No** |\n| **C**     | Exon\u202f6, 80\u202fnt upstream of the normal termination codon | No exon\u2011exon junction downstream of the PTC (it is the last exon) | \u2013 | \u2013 | **No** |\n\n*Why B differs from A*:  \nIn A the PTC sits >50\u202fnt upstream of a retained exon\u2011exon junction (exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f5); the rule therefore flags the transcript for NMD. In B the same PTC is now only 35\u202fnt upstream of the **new** junction created by exon\u2011skipping (exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f6). Because the distance falls below the 50\u2011nt threshold, the transcript no longer meets the criterion and escapes NMD.\n\n---\n\n**2. Expected steady\u2011state mRNA abundance (first\u2011order decay, equal transcription)**  \n\nFor a first\u2011order process  \n\n\\[\n\\text{degradation rate}=k_{\\rm deg}= \\frac{\\ln 2}{t_{1/2}}\n\\]\n\nIf the synthesis rate \\(k_{\\rm syn}\\) is the same for all constructs, the steady\u2011state level is  \n\n\\[\nM=\\frac{k_{\\rm syn}}{k_{\\rm deg}}\\propto t_{1/2}\n\\]\n\nHence the relative abundance can be expressed simply as the ratio of each half\u2011life to the WT half\u2011life (180\u202fmin).\n\n| Construct | \\(t_{1/2}\\) (min) | Relative abundance \\(M/M_{\\rm WT}=t_{1/2}/180\\) |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| **WT**    | 180               | 1.00 (reference) |\n| **A**     | 24                | 0.13\u202f\u2248\u202f13\u202f% of WT |\n| **B**     | 165               | 0.92\u202f\u2248\u202f92\u202f% of WT |\n| **C**     | 170               | 0.94\u202f\u2248\u202f94\u202f% of WT |\n| **D**     | 155               | 0.86\u202f\u2248\u202f86\u202f% of WT |\n\n(If you prefer absolute \u201cunits\u201d, multiply any chosen WT value by the corresponding fraction.)\n\n---\n\n**3. Effect of UPF1 depletion on construct\u202fA**\n\n*Why UPF1 stabilises A*  \nUPF1 is a central component of the NMD machinery. When a ribosome translates through a PTC that satisfies the \u201c>\u202f50\u202fnt upstream of a downstream junction\u201d rule, UPF1 (together with UPF2/3) is recruited to the stalled ribosome and the associated exon\u2011junction complex (EJC). This recruitment triggers endonucleolytic cleavage of the mRNA and subsequent rapid decay. Removing UPF1 eliminates the ability of the surveillance complex to recognise the PTC\u2011EJC configuration, so the transcript behaves like a normal mRNA and is degraded only by the basal turnover pathway. Consequently the half\u2011life rises from 24\u202fmin (wild\u2011type NMD) to \u2248155\u202fmin (construct\u202fD).\n\n*Prediction for the truncated protein*  \nTranslation initiation rates are unchanged, and the truncated polypeptide is assumed to be intrinsically stable. Protein output is therefore proportional to the amount of mRNA available for translation. With UPF1 depleted the mRNA level of A increases about 6\u2011fold (155\u202f\u00f7\u202f24\u202f\u2248\u202f6.5). We therefore expect the steady\u2011state level of the truncated protein to increase by roughly the same factor (\u223c6\u2011fold) compared with the UPF1\u2011proficient situation.\n\n---\n\n**4. Translation\u2011elongation inhibitor (e.g., cycloheximide) added just before the decay assay**\n\nNMD is **translation\u2011dependent** because the surveillance signal is generated when a translating ribosome encounters a premature stop codon **while the downstream EJC is still bound to the nascent transcript**. The ribosome must pass the PTC, pause at the stop codon, and then the NMD factors (UPF1/2/3) are recruited to the ribosome\u2011EJC complex. An elongation inhibitor blocks movement of ribosomes beyond the start codon, preventing them from reaching the PTC and consequently from contacting the downstream EJC.\n\n*Qualitative outcome*:  \n- The PTC in construct\u202fA will never be \u201cseen\u201d by a translating ribosome, so the NMD trigger is not engaged.  \n- Decay of construct\u202fA will therefore slow dramatically, approaching the WT half\u2011life (\u2248180\u202fmin) rather than the NMD\u2011accelerated 24\u202fmin.  \n- In a time\u2011course experiment the slope of the decay curve for A will become much shallower after the inhibitor is applied.\n\n---\n\n**5. Cell lacking exon\u2011junction\u2011complex (EJC) deposition**\n\n*What the simplified NMD rule predicts*  \nThe rule states that a termination codon triggers NMD **if it lies >\u202f50\u202fnt upstream of at least one exon\u2011exon junction that remains in the mature mRNA**. The physical manifestation of an \u201cexon\u2011exon junction\u201d in the cytoplasm is the EJC deposited during splicing. If EJCs are never placed, the cell has no molecular marker to define a downstream junction, even though the splice junctions themselves exist in the RNA sequence. Consequently **the condition \u201c\u2265\u202fone downstream junction\u201d can never be satisfied**, and the transcript will not be recognized as an NMD substrate irrespective of the distance between the PTC and the splice sites.\n\n*Resulting mRNA abundance*  \nBecause NMD is disabled, construct\u202fA mRNA will be degraded only by the basal turnover pathway, giving a half\u2011life comparable to WT (\u2248180\u202fmin). Thus the steady\u2011state level of A mRNA will be close to that of WT (\u223c100\u202f% of WT).\n\n*Resulting truncated\u2011protein production*  \nWith abundant mRNA and normal translation initiation, the truncated protein will be produced at a level proportional to the increased mRNA pool\u2014essentially the same magnitude as for WT transcripts encoding full\u2011length protein (or slightly lower if the truncated ORF is less efficiently translated, but the problem assumes identical initiation rates). Hence **truncated\u2011protein output will be markedly higher than in the wild\u2011type NMD\u2011competent cell**.\n\n*Separation of reasoning*  \n\n- **From the simplified NMD rule**: absence of EJCs \u21d2 no downstream junction detectable \u21d2 no NMD trigger \u21d2 mRNA stabilised.  \n- **From the additional assumptions** (equal transcription, normal export, normal translation, stable polypeptide): the longer\u2011lived mRNA translates into more truncated protein; the protein\u2019s stability is taken as given, so its cellular level mirrors the mRNA level.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Construct | NMD substrate? (rule) | Reason |\n|-----------|----------------------|--------|\n| **A** | Yes | PTC >\u202f50\u202fnt upstream of retained exon\u202f4\u2011exon\u202f5 junction |\n| **B** | No  | Skipping removes the downstream junction; distance <\u202f50\u202fnt |\n| **C** | No  | No downstream exon\u2011exon junction (last exon) |\n\nRelative steady\u2011state mRNA levels (relative to WT):\n\n- A \u2248\u202f13\u202f%\n- B \u2248\u202f92\u202f%\n- C \u2248\u202f94\u202f%\n- D \u2248\u202f86\u202f%\n\nUPF1 depletion \u2192 loss of NMD \u2192 \u2191 mRNA (\u22486\u2011fold) \u2192 \u2191 truncated protein (\u22486\u2011fold).  \n\nElongation inhibition \u2192 ribosomes never reach PTC \u2192 NMD blocked \u2192 decay slows to WT rate.  \n\nNo EJC deposition \u2192 NMD cannot be triggered \u2192 construct\u202fA mRNA stabilised to WT level \u2192 truncated protein produced at WT\u2011like amounts.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multiple subquestions.\n\nFirst, restate rule: termination codon triggers NMD if it lies >50 nt upstream of at least one exon-exon junction remaining in mature mRNA.\n\nThus we evaluate each construct:\n\nA: PTC in exon 4, 90 nt upstream of exon4-exon5 junction. Since there is a junction downstream (exon4-exon5), distance = 90 > 50 => qualifies as NMD substrate.\n\nB: Same PTC, but exon5 skipped; mature RNA has exon4 directly joined to exon6. Distance from PTC to exon4-exon6 junction = 35 nt (<50). No other downstream junctions? After exon6 there is normal termination codon but that's not a junction. So condition not met -> not NMD substrate. Alternative splicing removed the downstream junction, moving it closer (<50) thus evading NMD.\n\nC: PTC in exon6, 80 nt upstream of normal termination codon. No downstream exon-exon junction because it's last exon. Therefore condition not satisfied (needs at least one junction downstream). So not NMD substrate.\n\nNow compute steady-state abundances proportional to half-life assuming equal transcription rate and first-order degradation. Steady-state amount M = k_transcription * t_half / ln(2)? Actually for first-order degradation, concentration = synthesis_rate / degradation_rate. Degradation_rate = ln2 / t_half. If synthesis constant across constructs, then M \u221d 1/(ln2/t_half)= t_half/ln2. Since ln2 constant, M \u221d t_half. So relative abundance equals ratio of half-lives.\n\nGiven WT half-life 180 min. Compute ratios:\n\nA: 24/180 = 0.133... ~13% of WT.\n\nB: 165/180 = 0.9167 ~92% of WT.\n\nC: 170/180 = 0.944 ~94% of WT.\n\nD: 155/180 = 0.861 ~86% of WT.\n\nProvide numbers maybe as percentages or fractions.\n\nThird question: Why depletion of UPF1 stabilizes construct A. UPF1 is core factor required for NMD; without it, NMD cannot occur, so transcripts escape degradation, leading to longer half-life (observed 155 vs 24). The truncated protein encoded by A will increase proportionally to mRNA abundance (assuming translation efficiency unchanged). So its abundance should rise roughly 6-fold (155/24 \u2248 6.5). Provide explanation.\n\nFourth: Treatment with translation elongation inhibitor (e.g., cycloheximide) before decay assay. NMD requires active translation; ribosome scanning past PTC and encountering downstream exon junction complex triggers NMD. Inhibiting elongation prevents ribosomes from reaching the PTC and downstream EJCs, thereby blocking NMD. Thus decay of construct A would be slowed, resembling WT half-life (~180). Qualitatively, measured decay becomes slower (longer half-life).\n\nFifth: Mutant cell lacking EJC deposition. Without EJCs, the signal for NMD is absent regardless of distance. According to simplified rule, NMD requires presence of at least one exon-exon junction downstream; but the rule assumes EJCs mark those junctions. If they are not deposited, the condition cannot be evaluated; effectively no NMD triggered. Hence construct A mRNA would be stabilized (half-life similar to WT). Truncated protein production would increase accordingly (more mRNA leads to more protein). This conclusion follows from the rule plus assumption that EJC deposition is necessary for NMD detection. Also note that splicing still occurs, but EJCs missing; translation proceeds normally. So answer: high mRNA abundance, high truncated protein.\n\nNeed to separate parts: which part derived from rule (no NMD because no EJC) and which part uses extra assumptions (translation, stability of protein etc.)\n\nLet's craft final answer with sections.\n\n"
